+The orders and degrees of its subconstituents are $(N_1,d_1)=(k,\lambda)$
+and $(N_2,d_2)=(v-k-1,k-\mu)$.
+
+We use the following observation. Let a regular graph have order $N$
+and degree $d$ with $d>1$ and $2d+2<N$. Assume that $N$ is odd or $4d<N$,
+and that none of $d,d+1,d+2$ divides $N$.
+A disjoint union of cliques of order $t$ has $d=t-1$ and $t\mid N$.
+Its complement either has degree zero or has $d\geq N/2$.
+Thus both forms are impossible.
+
+A generalised clique cover of $sK_t$ has order $N=2st$ and degree
+$t-1+x+Y(s-1)t$, where $x\in\{0,1,t-1,t\}$.
+If $Y=1$, its degree is at least $st-1$.
+If $Y=0$, the four values of $x$ make one of $d,d+1,d+2$ equal to $t$ or $2t$.
+Both $t$ and $2t$ divide $N$. These conclusions exclude a generalised
+clique cover. Its complement has even order and degree at least $st-t$.
+If $s\geq2$, this gives $4d\geq2st=N$. If $s=1$, the complement has
+degree in $\{0,1,t-1,t\}$, which is incompatible with $d>1$ and $4d<2t$.
+This proves the observation.
+
+For both subconstituents, $d_i>1$ follows from the displayed
+parameters. For $q=2$, we have $(N_1,d_1)=(155,42)$, which gives
+$2d_1+2<N_1$ and odd $N_1$. For $q\geq3$,
+\[
+4d_1<\tfrac{16}{3}q^5<10(q^5-1)\leq N_1.
+\]
+For every $q\geq2$ we have $k\leq q^7+q^5\leq 5q^7/4$ and
+$q^{10}\geq8q^7$, so $N_2\geq27q^7/4-1>5q^7\geq4d_2$.
+In each case with $4d_i<N_i$, the bound $d_i>1$ gives $2d_i+2<N_i$.
+
+For the divisibility conditions, put
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+a_1&=q^2-q+1, & r_1&=q^5-q^3+q^2-q,\\
+a_2&=q^3-q,   & r_2&=q^6-q^3+q^2-q.
+\end{aligned}
+\]
+Expansion gives $N_1=(d_1+1)a_1+r_1$ and $N_2=d_2a_2+r_2$.
+For $q\geq2$, we have $a_1<r_1$ and $r_1+a_1<d_1$, since
+$r_1-a_1=q^5-q^3-1>0$ and
+$d_1-r_1-a_1=q^4+q^3-3q^2+2q-3>0$.
+We also have $2a_2<r_2$ and $r_2<d_2$, since
+$r_2-2a_2=q^6-3q^3+q^2+q>0$ and
+$d_2-r_2=q^7-q^6+q^5-q^4-3q^2+q^3+q-1>0$.
+The remainders for divisors $d_1,d_1+1,d_1+2$ are respectively
+$r_1+a_1,r_1,r_1-a_1$. For divisors $d_2,d_2+1,d_2+2$ they are
+$r_2,r_2-a_2,r_2-2a_2$. Each is positive and smaller than its divisor.
+
+The observation now rules out all four forms in
+Hypothesis~\ref{ass:classification} for both subconstituents.
